      {/* Section 3: Accessibility & Technical Limitations */}
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Accessibility & System Reality</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight mt-1">Important Accessibility and Technical Limitations</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Why stylized Unicode characters must be used with care on professional platforms.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 not-prose">
          <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-card border border-hairline space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400 font-semibold text-sm">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z"/></svg>
              <h3 class="font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-xs">Screen Reader Interpretation</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              Screen readers (such as NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver, and TalkBack) rely on Unicode specifications. Because bold Unicode characters are defined as mathematical alphanumeric symbols, some screen readers do not pronounce them as regular words. Instead, they may vocalize each letter individually (for example, reading <code class="text-ink">𝗯𝗼𝗹𝗱</code> as <em>&ldquo;Mathematical Bold Small B, Mathematical Bold Small O...&rdquo;</em>).
            </p>
            <div class="p-3 bg-canvas-soft rounded-lg text-xs text-ink font-medium border border-hairline">
              <strong>Best Practice:</strong> Never format full sentences or paragraphs with Unicode. Restrict styling strictly to 1–3 word section headers or step labels.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-card border border-hairline space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-semibold text-sm">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M21 21l-6-6m2-5a7 7 0 11-14 0 7 7 0 0114 0z"/></svg>
              <h3 class="font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-xs">Platform Search & Keyword Indexing</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              LinkedIn&rsquo;s internal search engine and external search crawlers index standard ASCII characters. Mathematical Unicode characters do not always map back to standard letters in search queries. If a professional searches LinkedIn for <code>growth strategy</code>, a post written with <code>𝗴𝗿𝗼𝘄𝘁𝗵 𝘀𝘁𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗴𝘆</code> may not match the search index.
            </p>
            <div class="p-3 bg-canvas-soft rounded-lg text-xs text-ink font-medium border border-hairline">
              <strong>Best Practice:</strong> Keep all primary industry terms, job titles, hashtags, and author names in unstyled, standard plain text.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-card border border-hairline space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400 font-semibold text-sm">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9.75 17L9 20l-1 1h8l-1-1-.75-3M3 13h18M5 17h14a2 2 0 002-2V5a2 2 0 00-2-2H5a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2z"/></svg>
              <h3 class="font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-xs">Cross-Device & Font Fallback</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              While current iOS, Android, macOS, and Windows releases support mathematical symbols, older operating systems, corporate virtual desktop images, and specific third-party browser configurations can lack the necessary glyphs. In those environments, unsupported characters display as missing rectangular &ldquo;tofu&rdquo; boxes (▯).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-card border border-hairline space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-semibold text-sm">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M3 5h12M9 3v2m1.048 9.5A18.022 18.022 0 016.412 9m6.088 9h7M11 21l5-10 5 10M12.751 5C11.783 10.77 8.07 15.61 3 18.129"/></svg>
              <h3 class="font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-xs">Translation & In-Page Search</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              When international readers use in-browser translation tools (such as Google Translate), mathematical Unicode characters are frequently skipped or treated as uninterpretable symbols. In-page search (Ctrl+F) on desktop browsers also will not match standard keyboard typing against mathematical symbols.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Section 4: Practical Guide to Structuring a Readable Post */}
      <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl bg-surface-card border border-hairline space-y-8 not-prose">
        <div class="space-y-2">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Content Architecture</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight">A Practical Guide to Structuring a Readable LinkedIn Post</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
            Formatting cannot compensate for poor structure. A readable post respects how professionals consume information on mobile devices, organizing insights into clear, skimmable sections.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline-soft space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs flex items-center justify-center font-bold">1</span>
              The Opening Hook (Lines 1–2, &lt;140 Chars)
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              State the core premise, finding, or question immediately. Because mobile feeds truncate after roughly 140 characters, your opening two lines must establish what the post is about without requiring vague clickbait. Clear topic clarity gives readers an honest reason to expand the post.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline-soft space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs flex items-center justify-center font-bold">2</span>
              Short, Breathable Paragraphs (1–2 Sentences)
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              Dense walls of text are fatiguing on 6-inch smartphone screens. Keep paragraphs to one or two sentences, separated by clean single line breaks. This creates whitespace that allows readers to scan the narrative at their own pace.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline-soft space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs flex items-center justify-center font-bold">3</span>
              Structured Lists and Takeaways
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              When presenting data, steps, or multiple observations, use standard bullet points (<code class="text-ink">•</code> or <code class="text-ink">-</code>) or numbered lists (<code class="text-ink">1.</code>, <code class="text-ink">2.</code>). Lists break complex arguments into manageable, self-contained thoughts.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline-soft space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="w-5 h-5 rounded-full bg-primary/10 text-primary text-xs flex items-center justify-center font-bold">4</span>
              Clear Takeaway or Call to Action
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
              Conclude with a concrete summary, a practical next step, or a specific question to encourage professional discussion. Avoid generic engagement prompts like &ldquo;Agree?&rdquo; or &ldquo;Comment YES below&rdquo; in favor of questions that invite real industry perspectives.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Section 5: When to Use Ordinary Text vs. Stylized Formatting */}
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4">
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Styling Discipline</span>
          <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-ink tracking-tight mt-1">When to Use Ordinary Text vs. Stylized Characters</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">Why excessive formatting harms reading speed and undermines professional credibility.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 not-prose">
          <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-card border border-hairline space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-emerald-500 font-bold">✓</span> Always Use Ordinary Plain Text For:
            </h3>
            <ul class="text-xs text-muted space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
              <li><strong class="text-ink">95%+ of your post body:</strong> Standard typography is effortless to read and natively supported across all screens.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-ink">Core search keywords & hashtags:</strong> Ensures LinkedIn search indexers and algorithms can catalog your content accurately.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-ink">Company names, URLs, and mentions:</strong> Prevents broken handles, unclickable links, or corrupted brand names.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-ink">Full sentences and paragraphs:</strong> Avoids screen reader stuttering and visual fatigue.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-card border border-hairline space-y-3">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-primary font-bold">⚡</span> Use Stylized Formatting Strictly For:
            </h3>
            <ul class="text-xs text-muted space-y-2 leading-relaxed">
              <li><strong class="text-ink">Short section headers:</strong> 1–3 word labels such as <code class="text-ink">𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗳𝗶𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴𝘀:</code> or <code class="text-ink">𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆:</code> that introduce a new block.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-ink">Step indicators:</strong> Prefixes like <code class="text-ink">𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭:</code> or <code class="text-ink">𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮:</code> in a numbered tutorial.</li>
              <li><strong class="text-ink">Single contrast words:</strong> Rare, selective emphasis on a single word where italic styling clarifies nuance.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft border border-hairline text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-ink">Why Excessive Formatting Backfires:</strong> When half of a post is bolded, italicized, or interspersed with decorative symbols, contrast is lost. True emphasis requires contrast against neutral surroundings. Over-formatted posts look visually aggressive, cause eye fatigue, and frequently evoke spam or artificial engagement tactics, reducing reader trust.
        </div>
      </div>

